WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.841 --> 00:00:03.395
Trust but verify is an old Russian proverb,

2
00:00:03.534 --> 00:00:06.912
made famous by Reagan in arms talks in the eighties.

3
00:00:07.936 --> 00:00:11.744
It is the working rule with experts: trust their skill and intent,

4
00:00:11.871 --> 00:00:13.427
check the results that matter.

5
00:00:14.477 --> 00:00:18.575
Verifying is not distrust: experts check each other constantly,

6
00:00:18.726 --> 00:00:21.106
with code reviews and test reports.

7
00:00:21.806 --> 00:00:22.909
You ask the same.

8
00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:25.820
Check results, not methods.

9
00:00:26.552 --> 00:00:30.011
You can't judge a cable sizing, but you can ask who checked it,

10
00:00:30.232 --> 00:00:31.358
and where it is signed.

11
00:00:32.412 --> 00:00:35.245
You can't read code, but you can watch the test.

12
00:00:36.395 --> 00:00:37.614
The four-eyes principle:

13
00:00:37.753 --> 00:00:42.107
every important piece of work is checked by a second qualified person.

14
00:00:43.138 --> 00:00:47.502
Your job: make sure the second pair of eyes exists, not be it.

15
00:00:48.905 --> 00:00:52.678
Risk-based checking: check harder where a mistake would hurt most.

16
00:00:53.704 --> 00:00:57.384
Safety, money and anything you can't undo: a full check.

17
00:00:58.459 --> 00:01:01.605
Routine gets a spot check: a small random sample,

18
00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:03.869
such as three of the forty drawings.

19
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.096
Avoid two failures: checking nothing,

20
00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.718
and everything, so experts feel watched and slow down.

21
00:01:12.077 --> 00:01:16.802
Ask without offending: about the process, not the person, and said upfront.

22
00:01:17.889 --> 00:01:22.556
On every project I ask to see the test results for the safety items.

23
00:01:23.273 --> 00:01:24.411
It's not about you.

24
00:01:25.415 --> 00:01:29.665
Upfront, it is routine; after a problem, it feels like suspicion.

25
00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:33.093
Checking only once you distrust is too late.

26
00:01:34.171 --> 00:01:36.818
Experts push back: no time for paperwork.

27
00:01:37.559 --> 00:01:39.718
Often fair, some checks are ritual.

28
00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:43.625
Ask which ones catch problems, and keep those.

29
00:01:44.673 --> 00:01:48.970
Sharp question: if this were wrong, how and how soon would we find out?

30
00:01:49.756 --> 00:01:50.522
At go-live?

31
00:01:51.302 --> 00:01:52.695
Then check before that.

32
00:01:54.058 --> 00:01:59.236
A robot cell: forty items on the supplier's checklist, none you can judge yourself.

33
00:02:00.216 --> 00:02:03.954
Step one, with the client's safety engineer: sort by risk.

34
00:02:04.654 --> 00:02:08.740
Five safety, three contract performance, such as speed and uptime,

35
00:02:08.926 --> 00:02:10.099
thirty-two routine.

36
00:02:11.107 --> 00:02:13.824
Safety: emergency stops, the light curtain,

37
00:02:14.021 --> 00:02:16.947
a beam that stops the robot if someone reaches in,

38
00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:20.268
and fence interlocks that stop it if a gate opens.

39
00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:24.058
Step two: safety and performance in full.

40
00:02:24.764 --> 00:02:27.921
You watch the test, the safety engineer signs.

41
00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.818
Step three: you see three random routine items.

42
00:02:32.877 --> 00:02:35.733
Step four: agree it with the supplier upfront.

43
00:02:36.473 --> 00:02:38.911
This is how I check, on every project.

44
00:02:39.623 --> 00:02:42.839
Grip on the eight that matter, a light touch on the rest.

45
00:02:44.198 --> 00:02:49.133
A red flag is a warning sign that something is wrong, before it shows in the plan.

46
00:02:50.125 --> 00:02:55.327
You can't see the technical problem, only its signals: how people talk and report.

47
00:02:56.367 --> 00:03:01.081
Three cover a lot: always almost done, vague answers, and no demo.

48
00:03:02.091 --> 00:03:05.876
The ninety-ninety rule, credited to Tom Cargill of Bell Labs,

49
00:03:06.061 --> 00:03:10.392
popularised by Jon Bentley in 1985, jokes:

50
00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:15.420
the first 90 percent of the code takes 90 percent of the time.

51
00:03:16.068 --> 00:03:18.553
The last 10 percent takes the other 90.

52
00:03:19.562 --> 00:03:24.403
If the percentage barely moves for weeks, ask what is left, item by item,

53
00:03:24.612 --> 00:03:25.843
and which is hardest.

54
00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:30.986
Vague answers: we're working on it, some minor issues.

55
00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:33.917
One is normal; a pattern is a signal.

56
00:03:34.905 --> 00:03:37.958
Ask which issues, and the next step on each.

57
00:03:38.673 --> 00:03:42.435
Someone in control answers in a sentence; others get vaguer.

58
00:03:43.533 --> 00:03:47.155
A watermelon status: green outside, red inside.

59
00:03:47.906 --> 00:03:50.785
Reported fine, while the team knows it isn't.

60
00:03:52.178 --> 00:03:56.276
A demo is showing the thing working, live, not slides about it.

61
00:03:57.355 --> 00:04:00.699
If a team building for months can't show anything working,

62
00:04:00.838 --> 00:04:02.870
that is the strongest flag of the three.

63
00:04:03.872 --> 00:04:07.599
Other signals: one person who knows it all and won't share,

64
00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:12.638
changing contacts, tests that keep slipping, bad news the day before a deadline.

65
00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.390
Careful: one flag is a reason to ask, not proof.

66
00:04:18.115 --> 00:04:20.634
Experts are often honestly optimistic.

67
00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:25.575
Respond with questions and evidence, not accusations.

68
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:29.537
And look at yourself: if bad news comes late,

69
00:04:29.688 --> 00:04:32.184
maybe people don't feel safe bringing it.

70
00:04:33.193 --> 00:04:36.862
A sharp question: what are you worried about that you haven't told me yet?

71
00:04:38.232 --> 00:04:40.751
An MES project, month 4.

72
00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.172
For three weeks the supplier reports the label module almost done,

73
00:04:45.323 --> 00:04:46.647
a few small issues.

74
00:04:47.393 --> 00:04:48.310
Status green.

75
00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:53.153
Last Friday's demo moved: the test environment was down.

76
00:04:53.885 --> 00:04:57.356
And the developer who built it just left for another client.

77
00:04:58.445 --> 00:05:03.994
At least 4 red flags: almost done for weeks, vague small issues, a postponed demo,

78
00:05:04.238 --> 00:05:05.445
the key person leaving.

79
00:05:06.232 --> 00:05:08.600
That green status may be a watermelon.

80
00:05:09.664 --> 00:05:13.310
This week: the open issues as a list, with owner and date.

81
00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:19.060
The demo on a fixed day, on any environment that works, even a laptop.

82
00:05:20.182 --> 00:05:23.526
Ask who owns the module now and how the handover went.

83
00:05:24.269 --> 00:05:27.833
Tell the sponsor: amber until the demo, with the reasons.
